{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1459969160,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUxNDY0MDM1MzQ=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/395","origin":"github"},"title":"What is our markup convention for variable references in the documention?","message":"I can see\n1. VARIABLE\n2. `variable`\n3. `variable`\n\nFrom the RST perspective the 3. is the appropriate choice (e.g. exercised by nibabel): [literal vs interpreted](http://docutils.sourceforge.net/docs/ref/rst/restructuredtext.html#interpreted-text)\n\nI was personally lazy and used 2 -- it is conceptually wrong, but is usually rendered the same way. I don't know what the motivation for uppercasing is.\n\nIn any case, we should agree on one style and stop mixing.","files":null}]}